There are $9$ coins and the first coin can be chosen in $\binom{9}{1}$ ways. Next, the second coin can be chosen in $\binom{8}{1}$ ways and finally, the last coin can be chosen in $\binom{7}{1}$ ways. This is because repetition of a coin is not allowed. When three coins have been chosen, it will not matter which of each type ($3$ quarters, $3$ nickels, $3$ pennies) is chosen. So, to find the number of ways to select three coins, one should construct a new situation such that the coin type choices only matters i.e., , say, A for quarter, B for nickel, C for penny. The number of three-element subsets of $\{A,B,C\}$ can be selected in $\binom{3}{3}$ ways. The number of ways to select three coins, $x$, which add up to $35$ cents is $35=\binom{3}{0}(3) + \binom{3}{1}(5)+\binom{3}{2}(25)$. This is because the only combination of quarter, nickel, and penny that will add up to $35$ cents is to select one quarter, one nickel, and one penny. The number of outcomes will be $x\cdot \binom{9}{3}$ ways. There are $(3) \cdot (3) \cdot (3)$ ways of selecting one of each in order. Finally, the probability that the value of three amounts of coins will be $35$ cents is given by \begin{align*}
\frac{(3) \cdot (3) \cdot (3)}{\binom{9}{3}} = \frac{27}{\binom{9}{3} } = \frac{27}{84} = \frac{9}{28}
\end{align*}. The probability that Steve has three quarters, three nickels, and three pennies is $\frac{9}{28}$.
